        <span>The One Nation network investigation</span>
        <strong>Exclusive</strong>
      </a>
      <a href={evidenceRoom}>Evidence Room</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="article-kicker"><span>Investigations</span><span>Politics</span><span>Exclusive</span></p>
      <h1>One Nation's astroturf news network was bigger than reported</h1>
      <p class="article-dek">
        The operation was not limited to six sites. A live North Carolina outlet promoted with Senate Leadership Fund money and a One Nation-branded Michigan staging build extend the footprint into all eight states named in SLF's $342 million Senate plan.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">By Augustine Bayer, Editor in chief</p>
      <p class="timestamps">Publication date pending</p>
    </header>

    <section class="investigation-facts" aria-label="Key findings">
      <div><strong>8</strong><span>states in the visible production footprint</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>additional properties documented by Boho News</span></div>
      <div><strong>14 of 59</strong><span>dated articles preceded domain registration</span></div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="standfirst">The political astroturfing operation first reported as six state-branded news websites had a larger visible production footprint.</p>

      <p>Boho News recovered two additional properties in the same narrow technical environment: The North Carolinian, a live site whose advertisements identified Senate Leadership Fund PAC as the payer, and Michigander News, a One Nation-branded staging draft hosted within a Senate Leadership Fund development environment.</p>

      <p>We use <strong>astroturfing</strong> in its ordinary political-communications sense: centrally sponsored advocacy made to resemble independent, locally rooted public sentiment or journalism. That description fits this network. Its properties called themselves independent state news publications, adopted local names and branding, and published candidate-favorable or opponent-negative political material, while their national political sponsorship appeared in legal footers, privacy-policy residue or advertising disclosures rather than in the ordinary presentation of the articles.</p>

      <p>“Astroturfing” here describes the form and provenance of the communication. It is not, by itself, a claim that a particular disclosure violated election law.</p>

      <p>Together with Alaska Navigator, Maine Policy Updates, Iowa Voice, Ohio Pulse, Peach State Post and New Hampshire Daily, the properties cover Alaska, Georgia, Iowa, Maine, Michigan, New Hampshire, North Carolina and Ohio. Those are the same eight states in which <a href="https://senateleadershipfund.org/press-releases/slf-announces-initial-342-million-investment-in-2026-senate-races/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Senate Leadership Fund announced an initial $342 million investment</a> on April 6.</p>

      <p>That geographic identity is significant context. It is not evidence that the announced $342 million paid for the websites, and Boho News found no invoice assigning a particular payment to a particular domain.</p>

      <p>The investigation also found that five of the six originally reported sites display some articles dated before their domains were registered. Fourteen of 59 dated articles fall into that category. Comparisons with identified source material support a narrower explanation than random fabrication: later site builds often retained dates associated with earlier source material, producing archives that appeared to extend further into the past than the domains themselves.</p>

      <p>That is evidence of retrospective population. By itself, it does not establish what operators intended the displayed dates to communicate or that anyone meant to deceive readers.</p>

      <aside class="source-callout evidence-room-callout">
        <p class="label">See every receipt</p>
        <p>Boho News froze the underlying pages, ads and records before publication. The Evidence Room holds the full-resolution figures, methods, public manifests, hashes, verification files and independent archive links.</p>
        <a href={evidenceRoom}>Open the One Nation Evidence Room →</a>
      </aside>

      <h2>From six reported sites to an eight-state footprint</h2>

      <p><a href="https://popular.info/p/senate-republicans-launch-network" rel="noopener noreferrer">Popular Information reported on August 6</a> that One Nation was operating at least six state-branded sites in competitive Senate states. It identified the properties through disclosures associated with Meta advertising.</p>

      <p>Boho News began with those six as a calibration set. We looked for candidates across advertising records, certificate data, development hosts, WordPress endpoints, analytics identifiers, theme residue, domain-registration records, archives, vendor portfolios and repeated language. A candidate was not attributed merely because it used the same registrar, nameserver, WordPress theme or vendor.</p>

