      <p>All eight shared a narrow production cluster that included Google Analytics property <code>G-MB5F8297KV</code>, the same WordPress child theme and an <code>acquire</code> production-author identity. The recovered installations occupied low-numbered slots in the same broader environment.</p>

      <p>Those markers were treated as corroboration, not standalone attribution. Our control searches found some generic infrastructure conventions across unrelated Acquire Digital work. The North Carolina and Michigan findings are stronger because direct payer or branding evidence converges with the technical evidence.</p>

      <h2>What the astroturf network was selling</h2>

      <p>The network did not merely aggregate neutral state news. Its own pages show a repeated political function: give Republican Senate candidates the authority and visual language of local journalism, amplify favorable issue frames, and publish negative frames about opponents.</p>

      <p>The clearest example is Alaska Navigator. Its homepage called the outlet “INDEPENDENT NEWS COVERING THE LAST FRONTIER,” but the legal footer said it was paid for by One Nation. The captured homepage led with three favorable pieces about incumbent Republican Sen. Dan Sullivan:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>“Senator Dan S. Sullivan’s Legislative Record of Supporting Alaska’s Veterans”;</li>
        <li>“Sen. Sullivan’s tax policy helps Alaska businesses thrive”; and</li>
        <li>“Opinion: Dan Sullivan has earned another term by delivering for Alaska.”</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The same homepage listed favorable pieces crediting Sullivan with tougher fentanyl enforcement, protecting Alaska Native fur handicrafts, military investment, fisheries advocacy and health-care funding. That selection portrayed Sullivan as a pro-military, pro-resource-development, business-friendly incumbent focused on veterans, public safety, fisheries and federal investment. Those themes overlap with <a href="https://www.sullivan.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/sullivan-touts-alaska-comeback-historic-opportunities-in-annual-address-to-legislature" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sullivan’s official account of his agenda</a>.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
