      <p>The pattern extended beyond Sullivan and Collins:</p>

      <figure class="article-data-module">
        <figcaption>Representative preserved political framing</figcaption>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">State outlet</th><th scope="col">Candidate or opponent</th><th scope="col">What the outlet presented</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Iowa Voice</th><td>Republican Rep. Ashley Hinson</td><td>Favorable coverage of spending, veterans, child care, farmers and consumers.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Ohio Pulse</th><td>Republican Sen. Jon Husted</td><td>Favorable coverage of military funding, East Toledo investment and anti-trafficking legislation.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">The North Carolinian</th><td>Republican Michael Whatley and Democratic opponent Roy Cooper</td><td>A favorable Whatley item and a promoted lead article attacking Cooper’s prisoner-release record.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">New Hampshire Daily</th><td>Democratic Rep. Chris Pappas</td><td>Opponent-negative tax and ethics frames.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Peach State Post</th><td>Democratic Sen. Jon Ossoff</td><td>A tax-vote frame that tracked separate One Nation advocacy against Ossoff.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <p>This is not a statistical sentiment analysis of every item and does not mean that every sentence on every site was false. Astroturfing can use accurate facts and real public records. The identifying feature is the disguised source and coordinated political purpose: national advocacy was packaged as independent-looking local publications whose editorial choices closely tracked the organizations’ Senate priorities.</p>

      <h2>North Carolina: the strongest new finding</h2>

      <p>The North Carolinian is the clearest extension of the public network.</p>

      <p>Preserved Meta records for the page identified SLF PAC as payer for 24 advertisements totaling $17,283 in the available all-time report. Decoded advertisement destinations led to a North Carolinian article. The captured site footer named Senate Leadership Fund, while the privacy policy retained language identifying One Nation.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={5}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-01-north-carolinian-homepage-and-slf-footer.png`}
        alt="Full North Carolinian homepage ending in the SLF PAC payer footer."
        width={765}
        height={4063}
        focus="bottom"
        caption="Unaltered full-page capture of The North Carolinian. The visible footer reads: “PAID FOR BY SLF PAC. NOT AUTHORIZED BY ANY CANDIDATE OR CANDIDATE’S COMMITTEE. WWW.SENATELEADERSHIPFUND.ORG.” The same capture includes a favorable Michael Whatley item and several negative frames about Roy Cooper."
      />

      <p>The privacy page made the cross-organization residue visible in its first sentence. Under a North Carolinian masthead, it began: “At ONE NATION [‘Organization’ or ‘we’], we are committed to protecting the privacy of our online guests.” It later referred to <code>thenorthcarolinian.org</code> and ended with the SLF PAC payer footer. That is not merely “similar language”; it is a captured North Carolinian page that expressly names One Nation as the organization.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={6}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-02-north-carolinian-one-nation-privacy-residue.png`}
        alt="North Carolinian privacy policy beginning with At ONE NATION and ending with the SLF PAC footer."
        width={765}
        height={8023}
        focus="top"
        caption="Unaltered full-page capture of The North Carolinian privacy policy, showing One Nation in the body and SLF PAC in the footer."
      />

      <p>The preserved Meta Ad Library screen showed approximately 29 North Carolinian results and 250,000–300,000 aggregate impressions at the time of capture. The corresponding preserved accessibility-tree record for Library ID <code>2060778401186867</code> displayed “Paid for by SLF PAC” and linked to the North Carolinian prisoner-release article. Three additional expanded cards in that capture carried the same payer and destination. The all-time spending report, which groups creative variants differently from the visible cards, recorded 24 advertisements and $17,283.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={7}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-03-meta-ad-library-north-carolinian-results.png`}
        alt="Meta Ad Library result screen for The North Carolinian showing approximately 29 results and 250,000 to 300,000 impressions."
        width={1280}
        height={720}
        focus="center"
        fullPage={false}
        caption="Preserved Meta Ad Library screen for The North Carolinian. The screenshot establishes the page, result count and impression range; the separately hashed DOM record preserves the expanded card’s SLF PAC payer line, Library ID and destination URL."
      />

      <p>The linked article framed former Democratic governor and 2026 Senate candidate Roy Cooper’s prisoner-release agreement as a public-safety failure. The capture displayed the same SLF PAC footer as the homepage.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={8}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-04-north-carolinian-slf-ad-destination.png`}
        alt="North Carolinian article about Roy Cooper prisoner releases reached from an SLF PAC Meta advertisement."
        width={765}
        height={2602}
        focus="top"
        caption="Unaltered full-page capture of the North Carolinian article reached from the preserved SLF PAC advertisement destination."
      />

      <p>The site also used the shared analytics property, the same WordPress child theme, the <code>acquire</code> author identity and deployment slot 14. Its domain was registered on March 20 within 2.002 seconds of the Alaska Navigator and Peach State Post domains, using the same registrar and nameserver pair.</p>

      <p>Registrar and nameserver overlap alone would not establish a relationship. Here it accompanies direct payer and branding evidence plus several independently specific production markers.</p>

      <p>The evidence supports two conclusions: <strong>SLF PAC paid for advertisements promoting The North Carolinian, and the site was a technical sibling in the One Nation/SLF production environment.</strong> It does not resolve every layer of legal ownership, commissioning, editorial control or article authorship.</p>

      <h2>Michigan: a real staging draft, not a proven launch</h2>

      <p>Michigander News was not a guessed brand or an extrapolated domain name.</p>

      <p>Boho News preserved a staging property under <code>slfsites.com</code>, an installation that identified itself as “Senate Leadership Fund Sites.” Captured footer and privacy material named One Nation. The property shared the network's analytics, theme and production-author residue, occupied deployment slot 17, and included an asset label containing <code>SLF-Michigander_News</code>.</p>

      <p>At the relevant capture times, the homepage was password-protected. But <code>/news/</code>, privacy pages, feeds, sitemaps, REST interfaces, assets and 23 scaffold posts were publicly reachable.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={9}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-05-michigander-password-protected-homepage.png`}
        alt="Password form shown at the Michigander News homepage."
        width={780}
        height={900}
        focus="center"
        fullPage={false}
        caption="Exact viewport capture of the Michigander News root. It presented a password form rather than the site homepage."
      />

      <p>The publicly reachable <code>/news/</code> page was not an error page. It rendered a Michigander News masthead, dated story cards, newsletter forms, an “independent statewide publication” description and a footer stating “Paid for by One Nation.” The story cards contained placeholder Latin rather than finished political reporting.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={10}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-06-michigander-public-news-path.png`}
        alt="Publicly reachable Michigander News path showing dated placeholder story cards and the One Nation footer."
        width={765}
        height={2945}
        focus="bottom"
        caption="Unaltered full-page capture of the public Michigander News /news/ path. It shows what could literally be viewed without a password: branding, dated placeholders, subscription forms, an independence claim and One Nation’s payer footer."
      />

      <p>The public privacy page independently identified both the responsible organization and the staging hostname. It began “At One Nation” and named <code>michigandernews.slfsites.com</code> as the website.</p>

      <EvidenceFigure
        number={11}
        src={`${figureBase}/figure-07-michigander-one-nation-privacy-and-footer.png`}
        alt="Michigander News privacy policy naming One Nation and michigandernews.slfsites.com."
        width={765}
        height={7505}
        focus="top"
        caption="Unaltered full-page capture of the public Michigander News privacy policy. The opening names One Nation; the body names the slfsites.com staging hostname; the footer says “Paid for by One Nation.”"
      />

      <p>The 23 posts occupied contiguous WordPress IDs 10 through 32 and displayed dates from July 8 through July 23. Twenty-one displayed dates predated the first observed certificate for the staging host. Seven posts had modification timestamps clustered across six seconds, including five one-second steps. That pattern supports assigned or synthetic scaffold dates during a compressed build or import process.</p>

      <p>It does not establish why those dates were assigned.</p>

      <p>Google's political-advertising bulk data showed One Nation running statewide Michigan video creative from May 4 through May 10. The available bulk record did not expose a landing destination. The advertising corroborates Michigan as a program priority; it does not establish that an advertisement linked to Michigander News.</p>

      <p>Boho News has not established that Michigander News intentionally launched to the public, carried advertisements, reached voters or involved the Rogers campaign. The accurate finding is that <strong>a One Nation-branded Michigan staging draft existed in an SLF development environment, with a protected root and publicly reachable surrounding paths.</strong></p>

      <h2>Dates that precede the domains</h2>

      <p>Among the 59 dated articles on the six originally reported properties, 14 display dates earlier than the primary registration event for the relevant domain:</p>

      <figure class="article-data-module">
        <figcaption>Displayed article dates compared with domain registration</figcaption>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Site</th><th scope="col">Articles dated before registration</th><th scope="col">Longest lead</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Maine Policy Updates</th><td>3</td><td>103 days</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">New Hampshire Daily</th><td>3</td><td>519 days</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Peach State Post</th><td>1</td><td>428 days</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Alaska Navigator</th><td>1</td><td>70 days</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Ohio Pulse</th><td>6</td><td>83 days</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Iowa Voice</th><td>0</td><td>Six displayed dates still preceded the captured build and homepage chronology</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <p>For 30 of the 59 articles, Boho News identified a source with a parseable date. Thirteen of the dates displayed by the network were within one day of the corresponding source date, and 14 were within two days. Of the 14 articles dated before domain registration, seven had a parseable source date; five were within one day of it.</p>

      <p>That distribution more strongly supports later population that retained source-era dates than arbitrary date invention. It nevertheless leaves an important unanswered question: whether a date was meant to represent source publication, editorial assignment, import, or publication by the state-branded outlet.</p>

      <p>WordPress database records, revision histories, import logs and editorial instructions could answer that. Boho News does not possess those private records.</p>

      <h3>A two-source splice in New Hampshire</h3>

      <p>One New Hampshire Daily record shows a more specific production anomaly. Its title, displayed date, byline metadata and outgoing link align with a May 2025 Boston Herald story by Matthew Medsger. Its body aligns with a different January 2026 story discussing the transition into 2026 and material associated with Tim Dunn.</p>

      <p>The preserved WordPress REST record reports a May 22, 2025 display date and a July 2, 2026 modification timestamp. This establishes a two-source metadata-and-content splice. A production or copying error remains a plausible explanation; the record does not establish intent.</p>

      <p>Marc Grasso of the Boston Herald told Boho News that Medsger was a Herald employee when the reflected article was published and that the supplied link appeared accurate. Grasso said he could not provide the original publication time and found no request or permission record for use of that article. His answer does not rule out every possible license, syndication route or permission source.</p>

      <h2>The Acquire Digital trail</h2>

      <p>Public Federal Election Commission records show Senate Leadership Fund payments to Acquire Digital described as website design, website development or web advertising. The preserved rows include $10,000 on July 2, 2025; $6,500 on July 28, 2025; $5,000 on March 5, 2026; $2,400 on March 12, 2026; and $1,200 each on April 9, May 7 and June 4, 2026. Readers can examine SLF's filings through the <a href="https://www.fec.gov/data/committee/C00571703/?cycle=2026" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEC committee record for C00571703</a>.</p>

      <p>Six confirmed network domains were registered five to eight days after selected March, April or June payments. That sequence is probative but not dispositive. Three of the 2026 payments were identical $1,200 rows spaced at an exact 28-day cadence, which is also consistent with recurring service. The public filings do not name domains or allocate invoices to properties.</p>

      <p>Separate technical evidence points more specifically to a production relationship. The shared WordPress author was named <code>acquire</code>; its preserved identity data linked to <code>slfsites.com</code>. The Gravatar hash associated with that account resolved to an <code>acquiredigitalkyle</code> identity, while <a href="https://acquiredigital.co/who/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Acquire Digital's team page</a> identifies Kyle Burns as chief creative officer. That supports an Acquire-specific production-account inference. It does not establish that Burns wrote, selected or approved any article.</p>

      <p><a href="https://acquiredigital.co/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Acquire Digital describes itself</a> as a full-service digital firm serving organizations, campaigns and corporations, including political and advocacy clients.</p>

      <p>The combined evidence supports identifying Acquire Digital as a central production vendor. It does not establish the exact contract scope, commissioning client, article workflow, individual responsibility or payment-to-domain allocation.</p>

      <h2>One Nation and SLF: connected, but legally distinct</h2>

      <p>One Nation is a 501(c)(4) organization; Senate Leadership Fund is an independent-expenditure-only political committee. They remain separate legal entities.</p>

      <p>Current official pages identify Alex Latcham as executive director of both organizations. The FEC identifies Caleb Crosby as SLF's treasurer, and One Nation's latest preserved tax return identifies Crosby as its secretary and treasurer.</p>

      <p>SLF's current-cycle filings through June 30 recorded three non-memo receipts from One Nation totaling $70.74 million. Reciprocal filings showed approximately monthly SLF-to-One Nation payments described as rent and salaries. Those records establish a material funding and shared-overhead relationship. They do not allocate money to these websites.</p>

      <p>The same distinction applies to SLF's $342 million announcement. The recovered properties and staging draft map to the same eight states in that plan, down to all eight state names. The announcement describes a much broader election program spanning broadcast, cable, streaming, radio, data, direct mail, texting, field activity and voter turnout. Boho News did not find evidence that the announced total funded the sites.</p>

      <h2>How far the search went—and where it stops</h2>

      <p>Boho News calibrated its search by requiring each method to recover known properties before treating a negative result as meaningful. The investigation reduced 525,104 Meta advertiser rows to 208 target-state news-like pages; decoded 374 One Nation and SLF live Meta Library identifiers; accounted for the observed <code>acquiredig.com</code> and <code>slfsites.com</code> names and deployment slots; reviewed staging hosts, BuiltWith candidates, portfolio pages and the Acquire FEC vendor graph; searched 29 Common Crawl collections; and analyzed 1,332 HTML files and 29,376 extracted URLs.</p>

      <p>No additional public One Nation/SLF news brand survived that review. Adjacent sites were separated when attribution, analytics, staff, scope or chronology did not match.</p>

      <p>This is bounded completeness, not proof of universal absence. A private, deleted, renamed, password-protected or never-indexed property could remain invisible to passive research.</p>

      <p>Boho News also tested the hypothesis that the known operation underwent an observable mass scrub after the August 6 report. We found itemized changes in advertising and development endpoints, but repeated normalized comparisons found no substantive article-inventory or readable core-page change across the known eight properties. No known news property suddenly disappeared wholesale. Michigan retained its split state: a protected homepage with public surrounding routes.</p>

      <p>The record therefore does not support claiming a generalized scramble, scrub or concealment effort.</p>

      <h2>Responses</h2>

      <p>One Nation, Senate Leadership Fund and Acquire Digital did not provide substantive answers to Boho News's detailed questions before this preview was prepared. Their lack of response is not evidence supporting any claim in this article.</p>

      <p>The Dan Sullivan campaign told Boho News that it intended to respond and asked about Boho News. Boho News answered. As of this preview, the campaign had not provided a substantive response. We will recheck the mailbox before publication. Boho News has not established that the campaign knew of, approved, paid for or coordinated Alaska Navigator.</p>

      <p>The Boston Herald response is described above. Bangor Daily News sent an automated acknowledgment. WMUR and the Concord Monitor did not provide substantive responses. No inference about permission or licensing is drawn from a publisher's silence.</p>

      <h2>We froze the record—and we will keep watching</h2>

      <p>Before contacting the organizations named in this story, Boho News froze the pages, ads, public records and technical material underlying our findings. That preserves what we saw even if a site, footer, privacy policy, advertisement or staging page later changes or disappears.</p>

      <p>We will continue monitoring the properties. If a post-publication change is material to this report, we will document it and update our coverage. Readers who want the technical detail can inspect the public manifests, original figures, hashes, validation receipts and archive links in the <a href={evidenceRoom}>One Nation Evidence Room</a>.</p>

      <h2>What the evidence does not establish</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>That Michigander News intentionally launched, carried advertisements or reached voters.</li>
        <li>That any beneficiary campaign knew of, approved, paid for or coordinated a property.</li>
        <li>That reader or voter data was transferred to campaigns or political groups.</li>
        <li>That post-August 6 changes were intended to conceal the operation.</li>
        <li>That every retrospective date was deliberately falsified.</li>
        <li>That a particular Acquire Digital payment funded a named domain.</li>
        <li>That SLF's announced $342 million program funded these sites.</li>
        <li>That all source reuse was unlicensed.</li>
        <li>That no additional private, deleted or never-indexed property exists.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The strongest finding is narrower and more concrete: a state-branded operation previously reported as six sites had a demonstrable eight-state production footprint. The additional record consists of a live North Carolina site advertised with SLF money and carrying mixed SLF/One Nation residue, plus a One Nation-branded Michigan staging draft in an SLF development environment. The same record documents retrospectively populated archives and a production trail strongly supporting Acquire Digital's role, while leaving commissioning, editorial intent and campaign knowledge unresolved.</p>

